@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+# 9. Due times
+
+Date: 2026-11-15
+Status: Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The help desk promises response times by priority: 4 hours for urgent tickets, 24 for high, 72 for normal and 168 for
+low. Agents need to see when a ticket is due and which tickets are late. Production holds tickets that predate this, and
+releases must be reversible: 1.4.0 has to start on a file the new release wrote and show every ticket.
+
+## Decision
+
+We will give each ticket a `due` time: its creation time plus the response time of its priority at creation.
+
+- The response times are settings (`DUE_HOURS_URGENT`, `DUE_HOURS_HIGH`, `DUE_HOURS_NORMAL`, `DUE_HOURS_LOW`), with the
+  help desk's values as defaults.
+- `due` is set once. A later change of the priority or of the response times does not move it, and no request can set
+  it. It is not an audited field: it is derived when the ticket is created.
+- The data file stays at version 1 and a ticket record gains the optional field `due`. Release 1.4.0 refuses any other
+  version but ignores fields it does not know, so it still starts on the file and shows every ticket.
+- A ticket without `due` (every ticket of a 1.4.0 file) is given one when the store opens the file, from its own creation
+  time and priority, and the file is saved at once. This happens whether or not `DUE_TIMES` is on, so the file holds the
+  due times before anything shows them.
+- `DUE_TIMES=on` shows `due` and enables `GET /tickets?overdue=true` (open and in-progress tickets whose due time has
+  passed). Without it the API answers as 1.4.0 does: no `due`, and `overdue` ignored like any unknown parameter.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Deploy with the flag off, check the file, then switch on; roll back by starting 1.4.0.
+- 1.4.0 drops `due` when it saves. A later start of 1.5.0 derives the due times again from the response times then in
+  force.
+- The command line and the weekly report are unchanged: their columns do not include `due`.
